              {
                "annotations": [],
                "text": "Each training example demonstrates the following rule:\n- Two opposing borders of the grid (either left/right columns or top/bottom rows) are solid lines of two different colors.\n- Inside the grid, there are isolated pixels of color 1 (blue).\n- Each blue pixel projects a single pixel of the border color it is closer to, placed adjacent to the blue pixel towards that border (in the direction of the closer border).\n  - Specifically, if the borders are at the top and bottom:\n    - If a blue pixel is closer to the top border, place the top border's color in the cell directly above (towards the top).\n    - If a blue pixel is closer to the bottom border, place the bottom border's color in the cell directly below (towards the bottom).\n  - If the borders are at the left and right:\n    - If a blue pixel is closer to the left border, place the left border's color directly to its left.\n    - If a blue pixel is closer to the right border, place the right border's color directly to its right.\n\nPlease provide the held-out input grid to transform.",
